    <!-- 1. STATIC VS DYNAMIC (Indigo) -->
    <section id="static-dynamic" class="card theme-indigo">
        <div class="card-header">
            <i class="fa-solid fa-table-columns"></i> Static vs Dynamic Data Structures
        </div>
        <div class="card-body">
            <p class="mb-4">Data structures organize data for efficient access and manipulation. The main distinction is how memory is allocated.</p>

            <div class="table-responsive">
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th>Feature</th>
                            <th>Static (e.g., Array)</th>
                            <th>Dynamic (e.g., List/ArrayList)</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>Size</strong></td>
                            <td>Fixed. Must be defined at creation.</td>
                            <td>Flexible. Grows/shrinks at runtime.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>Memory</strong></td>
                            <td>Contiguous (blocks next to each other).</td>
                            <td>Chained (can be scattered).</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>Access</strong></td>
                            <td>Direct Access (via index).</td>
                            <td>Sometimes Sequential (depending on implementation).</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>Pros</strong></td>
                            <td>Fast extraction, no overflow checks needed.</td>
                            <td>Memory efficient (no wasted space).</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <div class="tip-box">
                <span class="tip-title"><i class="fa-solid fa-lightbulb"></i> Exam Tip</span>
                <p>Static structures risk <strong>Overflow</strong> (not enough space) or waste memory. Dynamic structures manage memory automatically but can be harder to implement from scratch.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 2. ARRAYS & LISTS (Cyan) -->
    <section id="lists-python" class="card theme-cyan">
        <div class="card-header">
            <i class="fa-brands fa-python"></i> Arrays & Lists (Python)
        </div>
        <div class="card-body">
            <p class="mb-4">Python uses <strong>Lists</strong> which are dynamic arrays. They can hold mixed data types and resize automatically.</p>

            <div class="grid-2">
                <div>
                    <h3>1D Lists</h3>
                    <pre><code class="python"># Creation
grades = [70, 85, 90]
empty = []

# Methods
grades.append(60)     # Add to end
grades.insert(0, 50)  # Add at index 0
grades.remove(85)     # Remove by value
val = grades.pop()    # Remove & return last

# Access
print(grades[0])      # Direct access</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h3>2D Lists (Matrices)</h3>
                    <pre><code class="python"># 3x3 Grid of Zeros
matrix = [[0 for i in range(3)] 
             for j in range(3)]

# Access: list[row][col]
matrix[0][1] = 5  # Row 0, Col 1
val = matrix[2][2]

# Traversal
for row in range(3):
    for col in range(3):
        print(matrix[row][col])</code></pre>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="tip-box">
                <span class="tip-title"><i class="fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation"></i> Common Mistake</span>
                <p>When creating 2D lists, avoid <code>[[0]*3]*3</code>. This creates references to the <em>same</em> internal list. Changing one row will change them all! Use list comprehension (as shown above) to create independent rows.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 3. STACKS (Orange) -->
    <section id="stacks" class="card theme-orange">
        <div class="card-header">
            <i class="fa-solid fa-layer-group"></i> Stacks (LIFO Theory)
        </div>
        <div class="card-body">
            <p class="mb-4">A <strong>Stack</strong> is an ephemeral data structure that works on the <strong>LIFO</strong> (Last In First Out) principle. Think of a stack of plates.</p>

            <div class="grid-2">
                <div>
                    <h3>Core Operations</h3>
                    <ul>
                        <li><code>push(item)</code>: Add item to the top.</li>
                        <li><code>pop()</code>: Remove and return the top item.</li>
                        <li><code>peek()</code>: View the top item without removing.</li>
                        <li><code>isEmpty()</code>: Check if stack has no items.</li>
                        <li><code>isFull()</code>: Check if stack reached limit (static only).</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h3>Real-World Uses</h3>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Undo button</strong>: Reverses most recent action.</li>
                        <li><strong>Browser Back button</strong>: LIFO history.</li>
                        <li><strong>Interrupt Handling</strong>: CPU saves current state to stack before handling interrupt.</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </div>

            <h3>Pseudocode Implementation</h3>
            <pre><code class="plaintext"># Push Operation
if isFull():
    print("Stack Overflow")
else:
    topPointer = topPointer + 1
    stack[topPointer] = newItem

# Pop Operation
if isEmpty():
    print("Stack Underflow")
else:
    item = stack[topPointer]
    topPointer = topPointer - 1
    return item</code></pre>

            <div class="tip-box">
                <span class="tip-title"><i class="fa-solid fa-lightbulb"></i> Stack Pointer</span>
                <p>The <strong>Stack Pointer</strong> always points to the top element (or the next free space, depending on implementation). If <code>topPointer == -1</code>, the stack is empty.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 4. QUEUES (Pink) -->
    <section id="queues" class="card theme-pink">
        <div class="card-header">
            <i class="fa-solid fa-people-line"></i> Queues (FIFO Theory)
        </div>
        <div class="card-body">
            <p class="mb-4">A <strong>Queue</strong> works on the <strong>FIFO</strong> (First In First Out) principle. Think of a line at a supermarket.</p>

            <div class="grid-2">
                <div>
                    <h3>Core Operations</h3>
                    <ul>
                        <li><code>enqueue(item)</code>: Add item to the <strong>Rear</strong>.</li>
                        <li><code>dequeue()</code>: Remove item from the <strong>Front</strong>.</li>
                        <li><code>isEmpty()</code>: Check if number of items is 0.</li>
                        <li><code>isFull()</code>: Check if queue is at capacity.</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h3>Real-World Uses</h3>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Printer Queue</strong>: Documents printed in order sent.</li>
                        <li><strong>Keyboard Buffer</strong>: Characters appear in order typed.</li>
                        <li><strong>Process Scheduling</strong>: CPU handles tasks in order.</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </div>

            <h3>Pseudocode Implementation</h3>
            <pre><code class="plaintext"># Enqueue (Linear)
if isFull():
    error("Queue Full")
else:
    rearPointer = rearPointer + 1
    queue[rearPointer] = newItem

# Dequeue (Linear)
if isEmpty():
    error("Queue Empty")
else:
    item = queue[frontPointer]
    frontPointer = frontPointer + 1
    return item</code></pre>

            <div class="tip-box">
                <span class="tip-title"><i class="fa-solid fa-arrows-spin"></i> The Circular Queue</span>
                <p>In a linear queue, dequeueing leaves wasted space at the front. A <strong>Circular Queue</strong> wraps the pointers around: when the rear reaches the end, it goes back to the start (index 0) if there is space.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- GLOSSARY (Dark) -->
    <section id="keywords" class="card theme-dark">
        <div class="card-header">
            <i class="fa-solid fa-book"></i> Keyword Glossary
        </div>
        <div class="card-body">
            <div class="table-responsive">
                <table style="font-size: 0.9em;">
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th style="width: 30%;">Keyword</th>
                            <th style="width: 70%;">Definition</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>Static Data Structure</strong></td>
                            <td>A data structure with predefined fixed size and elements stored in contiguous memory locations.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>Dynamic Data Structure</strong></td>
                            <td>A data structure that can grow or decrease at runtime; elements may not be contiguous.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>Direct Access</strong></td>
                            <td>Retrieving elements directly using their index (position).</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>Sequential Access</strong></td>
                            <td>Checking elements one after another from the beginning.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>Stack</strong></td>
                            <td>LIFO (Last In First Out) data structure.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>Queue</strong></td>
                            <td>FIFO (First In First Out) data structure.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>Stack Pointer</strong></td>
                            <td>Variable pointing to the top of the stack (or next free space).</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>Push / Pop</strong></td>
                            <td>Adding / Removing items from a Stack.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>Enqueue / Dequeue</strong></td>
                            <td>Adding / Removing items from a Queue.</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

